410→                           Erdős <a href="#cite-Er96b-849" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('Er96b','849'); return false;">[Er96b]</a> credits this to himself and Gordon 'many years ago', but it is more commonly known as <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Singmaster%27s_conjecture" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer ugc" target="_blank">Singmaster's conjecture</a>. For $t=3$ one could take $a=120$, and for $t=4$ one could take $a=3003$. There are no known examples for $t\geq 5$.<br><br>Both Erdős and Singmaster believed the answer to this question is no, and in fact that there exists an absolute upper bound on the number of solutions.<br><br>Matomäki, Radziwill, Shao, Tao, and Teräväinen <a href="#cite-MRSTT22-849" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('MRSTT22','849'); return false;">[MRSTT22]</a> have proved that there are always at most two solutions if we restrict $k$ to\[k\geq \exp((\log n)^{2/3+\epsilon}),\]assuming $a$ is sufficiently large depending on $\epsilon&gt;0$.
